<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #e9f5f9;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; color: #333;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Text: Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the concept of English as a global language to classmates, using relatable visuals like a world map to show countries where English is spoken (e.g., "English has become an international language, widely used in business to help people communicate.")
            Can summarize instructions for an English language workshop, using gestures to highlight key session goals (e.g., points to icons of travel and work, and says, "We'll practice English skills for work and travel situations.")
            Can clarify the importance of English in international communication, using bilingual support and visuals as needed to convey the message (e.g., shows symbols of different career fields and explains, "Knowing English can help in global careers like business and diplomacy.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by displaying a world map and asking students: "Why has English become such an important language for international business?" Students brainstorm countries where English is spoken, connecting it to global interactions. The teacher asks, "What does 'lingua franca' mean in the context of global communication?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a concept using simple terms. For example, pointing to the map, the teacher says: "English <i>has become</i> a truly <i>global</i> language. It’s a '<i>lingua franca</i>'—a common language—that helps people from different <i>backgrounds communicate</i> in business and diplomacy. Many people around the world <i>learn</i> and <i>practice</i> English for better career <i>access</i>." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "relevant" /'rɛl.ə.vənt/ and "necessary" /'nɛs.ə.sɛr.i/ and the reduced vowel in "communication" /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If English is a 'lingua franca', does everyone speak it as their first language?" (No, it's a common language for people from different language backgrounds.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'global language', does that mean it's only for traveling, or does it also apply to <i>workplaces</i> and <i>business</i>?" (It applies to many contexts, including workplaces and business.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short announcement/memo (approx. 100-120 words) from a fictional "Global Career Development Center" detailing an upcoming "English for <i>Workplaces</i> Workshop". The announcement uses <i>formal language</i> and includes workshop goals, dates, required <i>skills</i>, and how to register. It incorporates present perfect (e.g., "English <i>has spread</i> widely"), modals for necessity (e.g., "Participants <i>must</i> bring..."), and key vocabulary (<i>communication skills, cross-cultural, employees, online platforms</i>, etc.).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize cross-cultural communication needs and use formal language in workplace contexts by comprehending the instructions for the workshop.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this announcement?" (To inform about an English for Workplaces Workshop).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the workshop and mark them T/F (e.g., "The workshop is only for advanced English speakers." / "The workshop aims to enhance cross-cultural communication skills.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key phrases from the announcement (e.g., "improve <i>fluency</i>," "global <i>interactions</i>") with their descriptions.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining concepts and summarizing instructions, focusing on <i>Present Perfect, Modals for necessity, Indirect questions</i>, and key vocabulary (<i>communication, skills, fluency, cross-cultural, global, access, enhance, explain</i>). Pronunciation focus on /e/ and reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences summarizing parts of the workshop announcement or explaining general concepts about English, using a word bank with target vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "English ______ (become) a vital tool for <i>cross-cultural communication</i>." / "<i>Employees must</i> ______ (recognize) the need for clear instructions." / "Can you ______ (explain) what <i>resources</i> are <i>accessible</i> for the workshop?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Information Rephrasing:</b> In pairs, students receive a specific instruction from the workshop announcement (e.g., "Participants <i>must</i> register via <i>online platforms</i> by Friday.") and rephrase it for a classmate who missed it, using simpler terms or different modals (e.g., "You <i>should</i> register... it <i>could</i> be a good idea to use the website."). They practice asking indirect questions: "Can you tell me how we <i>can access</i> the registration page?" The teacher highlights the long /e/ sound in "explain" and the reduced vowels in "accessible".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Summary:</b> Students work in new pairs. One student (Student A) receives two key points about the importance of English in the workplace (e.g., "English <i>has become dominant</i> in business" and "<i>Proficient communication skills enhance</i> career <i>access</i>"). Student B receives instructions for a specific task in the upcoming "Workplace Communication Role-Play" project (e.g., "Students <i>should</i> prepare 3 key phrases for their role," "Teams <i>must</i> ensure clarity in their message"). Each student then takes turns explaining their information to their partner, summarizing concisely, clarifying any points, and answering indirect questions from their partner (e.g., "<i>Could you explain</i> why English <i>has spread</i> so much?" or "<i>I wonder if you could tell me</i> what the main goal of our role-play is?").</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative explanation/clarification session for the "Workplace Communication Role-Play" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form small groups (3-4 members). Each group is assigned a part of the "Workplace Communication Role-Play" project. One student from the group acts as a "Project Explainer" while others are "New Team Members" who need clarification. The Project Explainer must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain the overall goal of the "Workplace Communication Role-Play" project, highlighting how English <i>has become important</i> for global careers. (e.g., "Our project is about demonstrating effective <i>cross-cultural communication skills</i> in a business setting, which <i>has become necessary</i> because English is a <i>global</i> language.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize the specific instructions for their group's role-play scenario (e.g., "We <i>must</i> clarify details with a client from a different <i>background</i>," or "We <i>could</i> propose a new strategy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarify any potential ambiguities or questions from their "New Team Members" using indirect questions and bilingual support if absolutely necessary (e.g., "<i>Can you explain</i> what challenges we <i>might encounter</i>?" or "<i>I wonder if you could tell us</i> how to make sure our message is really <i>clear</i>?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use visuals (e.g., a simple diagram of the project steps, icons representing different communication tools) to aid their explanation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "New Team Members" actively listen and ask questions, prompting the "Project Explainer" to mediate the information. The teacher circulates, observing <i>fluency</i>, use of target grammar/vocabulary, and effective mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and peer assessment during the clarification session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students (in their "New Team Member" roles) use a simple peer assessment checklist to evaluate their "Project Explainer's" ability to explain and summarize. The teacher also uses a checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation of project goals and English's global role.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy and completeness of summarized instructions for the role-play.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of Modals for necessity (<i>must, should, can</i>) and Present Perfect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer indirect questions clearly and to clarify information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, including target sounds (/e/) and reduced vowels (<i>communication, accessible</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>global, communication, skills, access, enhance, cross-cultural</i>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained clearly today was... and why it is <i>relevant</i> to our project."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need to practice summarizing... because it's <i>necessary</i> for effective <i>communication</i>."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">6-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did mediating information today help you better understand the 'Workplace Communication Role-Play' project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies (e.g., gestures, simpler language, visuals) did you use or observe that made explanations clearer?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of mediating (explaining concepts, summarizing instructions) worked well? Were students able to use modals and present perfect effectively in their explanations? What adjustments are needed for reinforcing mediation skills, especially for tasks involving bilingual support or clarifying complex ideas? Were there any specific pronunciation challenges with /e/ or reduced vowels? This lesson helped integrate all previous skills into a practical project explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (Since this is Lesson 5, the connection would be to a new theme, or continued work on the 21st Century Skills Project itself, building on the explanations and clarifications made today.) This lesson consolidates the theme "What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?" and sets the stage for the actual "Workplace Communication Role-Play" project performance, which will require continued effective communication and mediation within teams.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">4-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>